<div class="eyebrow">Controlled product walkthrough</div>
<h1>SecuraK MVP Demonstration</h1>
<p class="intro">The current MVP demonstrates how incident information can be organized into an investigation and response workflow. It is designed to communicate the product direction and gather feedback as development continues.</p>
<div class="alert"><strong>Demonstration disclosure:</strong> This MVP uses synthetic scenarios and simulated response actions. It does not connect to customer infrastructure, monitor live systems, disable real accounts, or perform production containment.</div>
<h2>What the current demonstration shows</h2>
<div class="list">
<article class="item"><h3>Incident overview</h3><p>A consolidated view of example security incidents, including severity and risk information.</p></article>
<article class="item"><h3>Evidence timeline</h3><p>A chronological view of synthetic events that helps explain the example incident.</p></article>
<article class="item"><h3>Analysis and recommendations</h3><p>Rule-based analysis and suggested next steps for the selected demonstration scenario.</p></article>
<article class="item"><h3>Simulated response workflow</h3><p>A demonstration of policy evaluation, simulated action results and verification status.</p></article>
<article class="item"><h3>Audit activity</h3><p>A record of the workflow events generated during the demonstration.</p></article>
<article class="item"><h3>Customer-facing reporting</h3><p>A simplified incident view intended to communicate status, evidence and response progress.</p></article>
</div>
<div class="note"><strong>Development direction:</strong> Future stages may include hosted customer workspaces, real log ingestion, persistent incident records, controlled integrations and human-approved response actions. These are roadmap directions, not claims about current MVP functionality.</div>
